#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116351} :activity/id #uuid "cf42fdc1-dee5-53a6-abe0-79ec4ef7b900" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048420 :topic/id #uuid "9d6972e8-70d1-541f-8fd7-7896e8369046" :topic/math-academy-id 1691} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116351 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116507 :tutorial/content "A function $f(x)$ is said to be **differentiable** at a point $x=a$ if the derivative $f'(a)$ exists.\n\nIn general:\n\n- If a function is not continuous at some point, then it is not differentiable at that point either.\n\n- If a function is differentiable at some point, then it must be continuous at that point.\n\nFor example, suppose we are given a step function $y=f(x)$ that has a jump discontinuity at $x=3,$ given below. We will verify that $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=3$ by showing that $f'(3)$ does not exist. \n\n![](images/9f/9f91b034a27ce7fd2b432f0b094e4b40098247916b78f9c1b3eb64c7214f69b4.png)\n\nThe value $f'(3)$ is a limit, so to check whether it exists, we need to compute the left and right-sided limits.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(3)={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $3$ from the left, we have that $f(x) = 2.$ So the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{2-2}{x-3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, as $x$ approaches $3$ from the right, we have that $f(x)=1.$ So the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\frac{1-2}{x-3}={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\frac{-1}{x-3}=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the left and right-sided limits are not equal, the overall limit does not exist. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(3)={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3}=\\text{DNE}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "60845968-f9d5-58f2-9205-7c60bc949ed6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 332 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "62b549c4-9fa6-595c-9705-edecd57fab60" :step/math-academy-id 615 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116354}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116352 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115380 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114920 :question/id #uuid "511342eb-691a-57be-a7cc-f10ab430b575" :question/math-academy-id "e-131"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "794398c0-ffbf-5971-a411-f236607f56b8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050574}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341929}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function is Differentiable at a Point Given a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "7d001457-3a19-5fb6-b582-e4447e917a71" :step/math-academy-id 616 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116355}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116353 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115381 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114921 :question/id #uuid "68eb6b1f-955c-550b-9232-3c6115ac220f" :question/math-academy-id "e-132"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b4100f8-8a66-5243-b0b6-c36b9d17dac3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051076}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392595}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Functions are Differentiable at a Given Point From the Function Expressions"} :step/id #uuid "d3a114b6-40b8-5189-a0c2-30250448ba57" :step/math-academy-id 619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116354 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116514 :tutorial/content "If a function is continuous, that does *not* mean that it's differentiable!\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the function $f(x)=|x|.$ The function $f(x)$ is continuous everywhere, including the point $x=0.$ But is $f(x)$ also differentiable everywhere? \n\n![](images/18/18644551713e0c1d451cf27bd43f3cc8fc8d7064d7af79df3cd2070af782c25e.png)\n\nFor $x < 0,$ the graph consists of a downwards-sloping line with slope $-1.$ For $x > 0,$ the graph consists of an upward-sloping line with slope $1.$ So, the derivative of the absolute value function is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-1\\, & x<0, \\\\ 1\\, & x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the derivative of $f(x)=|x|$ does not exist at $x=0.$ This is because the limit of the slope as we approach $x=0$ from the left is not equal to the limit as we approach from the right.\n\nIn general, a continuous function $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=a$ if the function $f(x)$ has any of the following features:\n\n- A **corner point** at $x=a.$ For example, $f(x)=|x|$ at $x=0.$\n\n- A **cusp** at $x=a.$ For example, $f(x) = \\sqrt{|x|}$ at $x=0.$\n\n- A **vertical tangent** at $x=a.$ For example, $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ at $x=0.$\n\n![](images/28/28a6f3a424556e1e60e4959b03a5c38a1ff6a5d0efb110a0e5a4bfadc7072511.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "0df8723a-c6d6-562d-8cde-b44a47dd70bc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 335 :tutorial/title "Continuity Does Not Imply Differentiability"} :step/id #uuid "872a7f94-c8a5-5f55-a91f-39909aca384f" :step/math-academy-id 622 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116352}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116355 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115382 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114922 :question/id #uuid "de600cc4-19fc-53a7-babc-fc387eff718c" :question/math-academy-id "e-133"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522804}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable"} :step/id #uuid "4284ae9c-1207-5fcc-829e-569528302322" :step/math-academy-id 623 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116353}}] :activity/title "Continuity and Differentiability of Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115458}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176605} :activity/id #uuid "cac9bb11-d48e-5140-9653-cc117881f57c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048748 :topic/id #uuid "bad1cfe2-7639-5714-8417-2b8af7ece5cb" :topic/math-academy-id 3571} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176605 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176968 :tutorial/content "Under what conditions can we be sure that the global extrema of a function exist?\n\nThe **extreme value theorem** answers this question and states the following:\n\n> *If $f(x)$ is a continuous function defined on the closed interval $[a,b],$ then $f(x)$ always has at least one global maximum and one global minimum on $[a,b]$*.\n\nFor example, consider the function $f(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x-3)^2, \\qquad x\\in [2,5].\n$$\n\nNotice that this function satisfies the conditions for the extreme value theorem:\n\n- the function $f(x)$ is *continuous* on the interval $x\\in [2,5]$, and\n\n- the interval $[2,5]$ is *closed*.\n\nSince *both* conditions are satisfied, the extreme value theorem guarantees that $f(x)$ must have at least one global maximum and one global minimum.\n\nTo see that this is indeed true, let's sketch the graph of $f(x).$ \n\n![](images/d2/d2ceffc40ead80b03d73004b663b91a942444bd14ce9e1e68fc98ffebbae3423.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the function $f(x)$ has \n\n- a global maximum at $x=5,$ and\n\n- a global minimum at $x=3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "300a3bb8-a79f-5387-8c5e-1e403c2df531" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2666 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9ff3fa5a-39c6-574d-bfa0-7e467bf44083" :step/math-academy-id 12791 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176805}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175768 :question/id #uuid "59426214-0bb1-5e8b-9e96-0fb84ec6361c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3732"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049593}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508954}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"} :step/id #uuid "8c5c661d-87d1-5fdd-b4cf-9b1217993d69" :step/math-academy-id 12792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176607 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175902 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175769 :question/id #uuid "2d31d7a1-bd6a-5794-a4af-b6cdad4dc64d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3733"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "205c5518-c67a-5784-8cc7-55b42ce7fb0e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050574}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522705}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether the Extreme Value Theorem Is Applicable to a Function Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "3001b2d1-c302-5b15-b3a6-3ff0fa36821e" :step/math-academy-id 12794 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176608}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176608 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175903 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175770 :question/id #uuid "1d90dec2-3c79-5069-ba1b-7e7e3f16ddb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3735"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051076}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341988}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "7889a404-286a-5541-b477-ed0966b909de" :step/math-academy-id 12795 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176606}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176805 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177036 :tutorial/content "We can only apply the extreme value theorem to *continuous* functions defined on *closed* intervals. Both conditions are crucial.\n\nTo see why this is the case, consider the function $g(x),$ given by\n\n$$\ng(x)=(x-3)^2, \\qquad x\\in [2,5).\n$$\n\nNotice that $g(x)$ is defined on a semi-open interval (the right endpoint is open). As a result, we cannot guarantee that $g(x)$ has a global maximum and a global minimum.\n\n![](images/a7/a7648d89fc02372f8d48a649425fa5dba4c8a86e12e9fa7bbddc69ffe52575c3.png)\n\nSimilarly, consider the function $h(x),$ given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}, & \\,2\\le x<3, \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+1, & \\,3\\le x\\le 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $h(x)$ is defined on the closed interval $[2,5].$ However, since $h(x)$ is not continuous on $[2,5],$ we cannot guarantee that $h(x)$ has both a global maximum and minimum on $[2,5].$\n\n![](images/b1/b1bdc0a3c384e5918bdac46ce5db31c0836069eb8a3383febf2aac86654929ea.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "08bf8574-c1f7-5521-983b-282f7b0c3cc3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5745 :tutorial/title "Conditions for Applying the Extreme Value Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "fa2543e5-fe3a-57fe-a964-118b017f51bc" :step/math-academy-id 35242 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176607}}] :activity/title "The Extreme Value Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176121}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509010} :activity/id #uuid "b940a17e-dd53-53d8-bf19-3b3ed1a866fe" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048748 :topic/id #uuid "bad1cfe2-7639-5714-8417-2b8af7ece5cb" :topic/math-academy-id 3571} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509003 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2fb7032-76c6-5840-a4b3-75b3bca0c64c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468022} {:answer/id #uuid "7da58ced-39e0-5469-8345-6d5c5c71fd19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468023}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468022} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f5aeadf-4637-56d4-8985-6b916361fe7c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468732} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d2b8f35-5eb0-5d60-99e3-d78e15538715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468024} {:answer/id #uuid "87c1ca6e-9c20-5182-95f4-352e35d95fb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468025}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468024} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b25706e-95ef-5012-a511-a60525ecadd8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468733} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4850f6e7-44e4-5c2c-ab1c-1fd4345196e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468026} {:answer/id #uuid "5d89fdc2-6b87-505d-9741-6111f42f5c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468027}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468027} :answer-field/id #uuid "bafe51e4-c08a-55a8-b1fd-0a9ee0fb0ef6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5aba1141-c0a4-54c1-bd64-30eb78786676" :question/math-academy-id "q-73484" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}?$    \n\nI. $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$\n\nII. $[\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n\nIII. $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$  is closed, but the function is not defined on a part of the interval because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$    is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ is  closed, and the function is continuous on $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "0ea7f73f-9a2c-5b1b-92bd-1284bb0cf34e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509024}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509010 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cc37e5f-628a-5453-b9aa-5f7736bbc9cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467590} {:answer/id #uuid "86c33542-1a37-5a8c-829b-f0ce4c00b10a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467591}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467590} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2c6904f-d234-579f-a1ce-7aceea241e6b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468516} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b766daf9-f662-5b41-bf41-146b6ff13df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467592} {:answer/id #uuid "ffeccce2-717e-5e80-b3c0-db1259995cf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467593}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467593} :answer-field/id #uuid "814f8eea-219e-55ac-a376-98a938bdbd22" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468517} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7babf862-7892-5b69-9b8c-2026a9d0a99b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467594} {:answer/id #uuid "61ed1c67-3d18-527d-aa8b-5e81455211be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467595}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467594} :answer-field/id #uuid "95cc6ec8-9bed-5ab7-9e0d-2ce973417c1a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a410e5f1-db4f-5d99-8a2f-cd16595bdd74" :question/math-academy-id "q-47923" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ \\,\\,1, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n- **I.** $[-1,1]$\n- **II.** $[0.5,1]$\n- **III.** $[-1,0)$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- Let first consider the interval  $[-1,1].$ This is a  closed interval, but we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1]$, and the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now consider  $[0.5,1].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0,$ which is not in the interval  $[0.5,1].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[0.5,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we note that the interval $[-1,0)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "47653334-ddc8-59af-8924-8f0b31ddb36e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509047}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509024 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341993 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08258f8b-dcaa-5fc6-ae65-f632b2ec32c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468052} {:answer/id #uuid "29791dee-07cd-517e-ade1-ff8025ea2d3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468053}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468052} :answer-field/id #uuid "e380e8d8-76cf-5f78-bbc9-d5f2bfff507e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468747} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fa6caaf-0d53-550a-81a3-65d8c87e7671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468054} {:answer/id #uuid "7b92b7c9-5e55-5053-bc30-ae2124f51f3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468055}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468054} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d7306f5-05ce-59d5-926c-66b49d14119f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468748} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52ffb37d-c85f-5070-8a42-00349cde98df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468056} {:answer/id #uuid "332ea8dc-cd67-5380-9b7b-ad3430d95bcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468057}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468057} :answer-field/id #uuid "3acf4f19-1ed8-5841-bd74-4e01d898c416" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7a761269-f6ea-50eb-8e45-58983c3d9692" :question/math-academy-id "q-73503" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-x}{{2}^{x}+1}, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ \\,\\,2, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n- **I.** $[-1,1]$\n- **II.** $(0,3]$\n- **III.** $[-2,-1]$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- First, let's consider $[-1,1].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2-x}{{2}^{x}+1}=\\frac{2-0}{{2}^{0}+1}=1\\ne f(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(0,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[-2,-1].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0,$ which is not in the interval  $[-2,-1].$  Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[-2,-1],$  and the extreme value theorem is also applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "98d94b5f-1594-5a4d-b5ff-4c0b24e893f7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509033}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509033 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508954 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3eb3bff0-d429-5165-9782-7f65936a7d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508480} {:answer/id #uuid "690abb71-4d77-510e-a85b-3cd428ff1d32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508548} {:answer/id #uuid "7f70533b-31ac-541b-be61-eba3464f5b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508576} {:answer/id #uuid "8795d4c5-ffa2-5bdb-8d59-fab4fdcfe7bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508592} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b2db0b-70aa-51ed-a1c5-b48983c58ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508639}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508592} :answer-field/id #uuid "0afcfbf6-047c-53c2-8a23-be6af1196c25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "91c6ce97-3ba3-5af9-8b62-e9a8d179a50b" :question/math-academy-id "q-131070" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{{x}^{2}-1}, & x<1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+1}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nI. $[-5,1)$\n\nII. $[0,5]$\n\nIII. $[5,10]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- The interval $[-5,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now, consider $[0,5].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{{x}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{x(x-1)}{(x-1)(x+1)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x}{x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{1}{x+1})=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[0,5],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, consider $[5,10].$ In this interval $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[5,10].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "bf9d0322-941e-5a54-b92e-f70ddfb5e71b"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509047 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61e532f7-a8d4-5ad0-87fa-e0abb07a53ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468010} {:answer/id #uuid "166b32ef-7d9c-5749-b3c9-7228a4faa2d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468011}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468010} :answer-field/id #uuid "11346c34-25dc-5818-adc6-68489744c4a9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468726} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcab39e9-589e-5993-9821-bf3f565fc3c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468012} {:answer/id #uuid "5faf65eb-1420-5b19-84fa-7b4d080f51e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468013}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468013} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd1c1baa-2550-538a-9bce-29f2173a21b4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468727} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "137588e4-2424-5d62-8fe6-5492472a9566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468014} {:answer/id #uuid "ec6eb95e-2a8c-5ac0-b216-c72fc2f4b9cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468015}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468015} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cf26ba3-fa7e-5c2b-86e3-65a942cdda6b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468728}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "122f233b-5382-5fef-8fbd-2b8682266e28" :question/math-academy-id "q-73481" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{x}}{{x}^{2}+1}?$   \n\nI. $[-1,5]$\n\nII. $[0,2]$\n\nIII. $[2,5]$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,5]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because $\\sqrt[4]{x}$ is not defined anywhere in the subinterval $[-1,0).$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The intervals $[0,2]$ and $[2,5]$ are closed, and the function is continuous on each of these intervals Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on each $[0,2]$  and $[2,5].$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "fb835e05-9455-5b93-ad9d-42052fd2df0a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509003}}] :activity/title "The Extreme Value Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508384}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101837} :activity/id #uuid "6f288c2e-8f4d-5306-9c16-f6582046db0f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049040 :topic/id #uuid "d56ad601-79c5-5201-9340-f03bd4da4f1f" :topic/math-academy-id 1262} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101837 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101931 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} f(x)$ for the piecewise function $f(x)$ given below.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<1 \\\\ 2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x=1 \\\\ -(x-1)^{3}+3\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by sketching a graph of the function:\n\n![](images/01/01e1704152432a01a1491f6d7575ada56907272e53f5eeee8a90b8bc96f3eb51.png)\n\nTo compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} f(x),$ we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x) = x^3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}{x}^{3} \\\\  & ={1}^{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-(x-1)^3+3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}-(x-1)^{3}+3 \\\\  & =-(1-1)^{3}+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the left and right-sided limits are not equal, the overall limit does not exist: \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} f(x) = \\text{DNE}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "80db4900-d9c5-5900-b975-5c26b8f713e2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 236 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d1483e1b-2de0-5e5b-a8d1-f4d81897891d" :step/math-academy-id 424 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101838}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101838 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100977 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100753 :question/id #uuid "62d73be9-5f67-5f44-a45a-f5c25a4dfe27" :question/math-academy-id "e-64"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0995bfd4-20b1-5f12-9d1d-fcb180a6291e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050454}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362398}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Coincide"} :step/id #uuid "51e2792d-7ab3-57f6-a8aa-4475d65fedf7" :step/math-academy-id 425 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101839}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101839 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100978 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100754 :question/id #uuid "e9a033d1-eb3f-5e7e-8237-346b03681499" :question/math-academy-id "e-65"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdd4a2e4-b0e6-54e8-972c-3dc194ce4ab3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522695}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Do Not Coincide"} :step/id #uuid "e1dad1df-a9ec-5939-84eb-1443d3e7f165" :step/math-academy-id 428 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101840}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101840 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101932 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0}f(x)$ for the piecewise function given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)={\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x+1}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get an idea of the behavior of the function around $x=0,$ we can plot the function.\n\n![](images/78/78a832c65a4bb5862694717f52704a61660bd81a34eb7a72cfeeb98a52bb50f1.png)\n\nTo compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} f(x),$ we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x < 0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3^{x+1}.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{3}^{x+1} \\\\  & ={3}^{0+1} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt {x^2+9}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe left and right-sided limits both exist and are equal. So, the overall limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} f(x) =3.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "416b850c-1c8d-5794-a787-3a59d48d0616" :tutorial/math-academy-id 239 :tutorial/title "Limits of Piecewise Functions"} :step/id #uuid "f2bafd4a-88b6-500a-b45d-6de37cdf6804" :step/math-academy-id 430 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101841}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101841 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100979 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100758 :question/id #uuid "7f534444-765b-5c3a-a381-883a48911700" :question/math-academy-id "e-66"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bc794c7-cd0c-5ebc-aa11-9b82e72905fd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048943}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402495}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Limits of Piecewise Functions with a Gap in the Domain"} :step/id #uuid "9a5952ff-0a94-5306-8005-3e5b53635c33" :step/math-academy-id 431 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101842}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101842 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100980 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100761 :question/id #uuid "baed0974-c209-5e29-99b5-d7f1d55b296d" :question/math-academy-id "e-67"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4014376-4dae-56f1-b981-b35d3afab874" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362399}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Constant Such that the Limit of a Piecewise Function Exists"} :step/id #uuid "bcca1c4b-c754-54c1-a87f-df2f0b306855" :step/math-academy-id 433}] :activity/title "Limits of Piecewise Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101099}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106812} :activity/id #uuid "fbe08d91-7f85-5aca-90e3-9537e43e9705" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049637 :topic/id #uuid "072aa5e8-f5da-53f2-8406-4aa8a1bc1e24" :topic/math-academy-id 1263} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106560 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107064 :tutorial/content "Constants do not vary with $x.$ So, any limit of a constant evaluates to just the constant itself.\n\nFor example, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} 3=3.\n$$\n\nLikewise, \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\sqrt{ \\pi}= \\sqrt{\\pi} .\n$$\n\nIn general, for any constant $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\pm\\infty} C=C.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "19052219-09a5-5548-a770-353adefe214a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2581 :tutorial/title "Infinite Limits of Constant Functions"} :step/id #uuid "45bc2482-5b40-5a90-b961-e785160dde91" :step/math-academy-id 12015 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106561}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106561 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105700 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105563 :question/id #uuid "3977d627-65bc-5f36-8c25-7d47b515f383" :question/math-academy-id "e-3566"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6456693-8845-5f7b-8b0b-2d6802bbf72e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348062}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Constant Function"} :step/id #uuid "96ffef5f-bd71-5fb3-8b87-d6ba58b7e210" :step/math-academy-id 12016 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106827}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107030 :tutorial/content "Multiplication of a power function by any *positive* real constant does not influence its limit at infinity. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }2{x}^{2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbecause $y=2x^2$ is an upward parabola, just like $y=x^2.$\n\n![](images/47/47d8cef2d3d189b32ee1c37f9fa563f58670d0bdc876360b13753106df03a79d.png)\n\nOn the other hand, multiplication of a power function by a *negative* real constant changes the *sign* of the limit. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-2{x}^{2}=-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2}=-\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbecause $y=-2x^2$ is a downwards parabola that points in the opposite direction as $y=x^2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6ae2ce56-a932-54d8-a6e9-9eea329c623b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1335 :tutorial/title "The Effect of Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "17d3a58d-07af-5d54-aca7-37b0e9e2f9b5" :step/math-academy-id 2627 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106813}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106812 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107038 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^2$ and $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^2.$ To do this, we can use the graph of $y=x^2,$ which takes the familiar shape of a parabola.\n\n![](images/58/58536c20f942b37e75a1ebb60fa6298af5fa94d1ba7b2b1cd770a0251796b436.png)\n\nOn the right side of the graph, we see that $y$ increases without bound as $x \\to \\infty.$ On the left side of the graph, we have a similar situation: $y$ again increases without bound as $x \\to -\\infty.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^2=\\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^2=\\infty.\n$$\n\nNotice that the functions $y=x^4$ and $y=x^6$ have a similar shape, and as such their limiting behavior is the same.\n\n![](images/5e/5e7386db15731b3486fd64484db69e7919c8e79821d916974f689d737cf23f8a.png)\n\nIn general, whenever $n$ is an *even natural number*, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^n=\\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^n=\\infty, \\qquad \\textrm{for}\\qquad n=2,4,6,\\ldots.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9981abcd-17f0-549f-9dcc-067b9bc17a0f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 190 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6a8c98da-eef2-5791-8a0c-e4700a11a8d6" :step/math-academy-id 353 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106747}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106813 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105849 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105564 :question/id #uuid "88c456d9-e5d3-5b33-a847-03f201751c19" :question/math-academy-id "e-36"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4974097a-95c9-5835-ba1d-46480efab66b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050259}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362355}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Even Power"} :step/id #uuid "096e64b1-f521-5ed3-8f48-cd30efc9ff5e" :step/math-academy-id 354 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106818}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106818 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107039 :tutorial/content "Now let's look at the graph of $y=x^3.$\n\n![](images/53/53d67c89fce03c6b949aecbe1e55fe8bf912ebadf1c3edc6d6c3601047886b5a.png)\n\nWe notice that the situation here is different:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} x^3=\\infty \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\lim_{x \\to -\\infty} x^3=-\\infty.\n$$\n\nThese limits are valid for any power function of the form $x^n,$ where $n$ is an *odd natural number*.\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^n=\\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^n=-\\infty, \\qquad n=1,3,5,\\ldots.\n$$\n\nAgain, multiplication of $x^n$ by a positive constant does not affect the limit at infinity. However, multiplication by a negative constant changes the sign of the limit." :tutorial/id #uuid "d9d4d60e-df4b-5dbb-85e6-e53ab24b16f2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 192 :tutorial/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Odd Power"} :step/id #uuid "c2cbae50-d421-561d-b4fe-f6dcc8a8591d" :step/math-academy-id 357 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106820}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106820 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105854 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105565 :question/id #uuid "551f2aa0-8589-5725-abe6-de1b7eca0327" :question/math-academy-id "e-37"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bea806-2913-57f5-ba32-be7aeda5bd15" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320929}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Odd Power"} :step/id #uuid "800c9745-b1c3-57bb-8946-69f8df02ae03" :step/math-academy-id 358 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106560}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106827 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107040 :tutorial/content "To compute the limit at infinity of a general polynomial, we *only* need to consider the term with the largest exponent, called the **leading term**. All of the other terms can be disregarded!\n\nFor example, to compute \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to \\infty} \\left( 6x + 2x^3-7x^2+12\\right) \\, ,\n$$\n\n we identify the leading term and compute its limit. Here, the leading term is ${\\color{blue}{2x^3}},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(6x+2{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+12) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reason this works is that when $x$ becomes very large, the behavior of the polynomial is dominated by the term with the largest exponent.\n\n**Watch out!** The leading term is *not* always the first term that appears in the expression. Rather, it is the term *with the largest exponent*. This term may appear anywhere in the expression.\n\nThe reason why we call the term with the largest exponent the \"leading\" term is that when the polynomial is arranged in standard form (with exponents ordered from greatest to least), the term with the largest exponent appears first:\n\n$$\n6x+ {\\color{blue}{2x^3}}-7x^2+12 \\quad \\to \\quad {\\color{blue}{2x^3}}-7x^2+6x+12\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "549fd7b8-2367-5c38-b26e-475d7c827a1e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 196 :tutorial/title "Limits at Infinity of General Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "5e48e500-3385-5631-9674-a6c6b177bfcd" :step/math-academy-id 363 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106828}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106828 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105857 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105576 :question/id #uuid "969d5f07-c5ce-5601-b2de-0d23eb15809b" :question/math-academy-id "e-39"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc04f720-28f3-55ab-8e89-582db57672a4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522796}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a General Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "923659ef-18ec-5fc8-8fb8-ff70ca00d5c0" :step/math-academy-id 364}] :activity/title "Limits at Infinity of Polynomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105950}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079227} :activity/id #uuid "c69d16f5-44d0-5069-b44e-d2caf86ea506" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049806 :topic/id #uuid "156024ea-0497-5260-b6dc-995afd569210" :topic/math-academy-id 638} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079227 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079683 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x)$ below:\n![](images/e4/e46fcaf79d93513c8b946de51ef451ad8a638936681ce3fc70ffdbe8de023078.svg)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ seems to have a jump discontinuity at $x=-3.$ However, by selecting a particular value for $k$, we can remove the jump discontinuity and make the function continuous.\n\nWe can find this value of $k$ by forcing the left and right-sided limits of the function to be equal at $x=-3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to (-3)^-} f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x\\to (-3)^+} f(x)\n$$\n\nFor the left limit, we have,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}(3{x}^{2}+kx) \\\\  & =3(-3)^{2}+k(-3) \\\\  & =27-3k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}{x}^{3} \\\\  & =(-3)^{3} \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the left and right limits as equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27-3k & =-27 \\\\ 3k & =54 \\\\ k & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=-3,$ we need to choose $k=18.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "10dabe2c-998a-5731-bcf2-c0ed93bc7f2e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 543 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "778b28a2-a45c-54f3-993a-34eba4c8d1b4" :step/math-academy-id 1021 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079229}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079228 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078382 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078220 :question/id #uuid "d17b0e14-aabc-5814-a242-d5fb803cb406" :question/math-academy-id "e-263"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55607d46-d93c-5306-9b2e-b9a8e79366eb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048784}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507466}] :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Radical Function"} :step/id #uuid "6bfe194f-fa72-57c9-8918-36cd3025c6c5" :step/math-academy-id 1022 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079397}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079229 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078383 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078222 :question/id #uuid "90e5fce3-26db-513d-9270-1e60b8dfb0d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-264"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "266c7322-6395-5e9b-ad8e-fe51f3c36665" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050124}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507471} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522761}] :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Polynomial Function"} :step/id #uuid "05ececba-94c0-5207-bb02-a5955fd09f40" :step/math-academy-id 1025 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079228}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079230 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078384 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078225 :question/id #uuid "b19955e9-3e5d-597e-81bc-f8f675e78e9b" :question/math-academy-id "e-265"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "237b4039-b516-54cb-ab4d-546578a0b1c2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049944}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507525}] :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Logarithmic Function"} :step/id #uuid "31a192d6-435a-5d0f-9c21-a362b860c2c8" :step/math-academy-id 1027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079397 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078490 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078334 :question/id #uuid "aef91e7a-138c-58bc-a25c-ca77284581c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-6279"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e155cb07-8d89-5d4b-9015-f73300bab2e9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050511}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507479}] :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Exponential Function"} :step/id #uuid "5f820e0e-c43c-5112-8821-d68f8ea15c85" :step/math-academy-id 23595 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079230}}] :activity/title "Removing Jump Discontinuities" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078661}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101570} :activity/id #uuid "4b7be0a1-49cf-57e8-8286-c52f0151df07" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049825 :topic/id #uuid "16e6877f-e43f-5432-a6fb-d2029db06718" :topic/math-academy-id 1225} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101570 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101995 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x)$ below:\n![](images/cb/cbdbe44a94ff036c74957691e30ed3652d50d2a8dbaa50b29827408f242492bb.svg)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ seems to have a point discontinuity at $x=1.$ However, by selecting a particular value for $k,$ we can remove the point discontinuity and make the function continuous.\n\nWe can find this value of $k$ by forcing the limit of the function to be equal to the value of the function at $x=1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x) = f(1)\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x)$ represents the value that $f(x)$ approaches as $x$ gets closer and closer, but not equal, to $1.$ So, when evaluating $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x),$ we need to use the function definition for $x \\neq 1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{2}+x-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)}{(x-1)(x+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)}(x+1)}{\\cancel{(x-1)}(x+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x+1}{x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{1+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=1,$ the function is defined as $f(x)=k.$ So, $f(1)=k$, and for continuity we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x) & =f(1) \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & =k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=1,$ we need to choose $k=\\dfrac{2}{3}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "81a7db1c-98c9-5308-96d8-457b407671db" :tutorial/math-academy-id 548 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d10fc2dc-3be0-5724-8892-7273ab4b7322" :step/math-academy-id 1031 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101571}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101571 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100803 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100618 :question/id #uuid "8148cf4c-eae5-54de-adb9-6b8fdf01c7a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-267"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2cd56ebd-30ee-5acf-b691-5c2e5f485b52" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049927}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522767}] :knowledge-point/title "Removing Point Discontinuities From Rational Functions"} :step/id #uuid "080a88b1-8b9c-503a-a435-862f18fa647f" :step/math-academy-id 1032 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101572}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101572 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100804 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100619 :question/id #uuid "837cbd93-6e15-537c-8661-37f42294e66f" :question/math-academy-id "e-268"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "583e8820-a8c9-5c94-84cc-d5ff1ee239b4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050511}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511525}] :knowledge-point/title "Removing Jump Discontinuities From Rational Functions"} :step/id #uuid "7867d9d8-2d45-52ba-b25f-cdcdfeac2305" :step/math-academy-id 1035}] :activity/title "Removing Discontinuities From Rational Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101077}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120903} :activity/id #uuid "1cbd6cd3-5c23-5011-83d6-29ca3fbd3d32" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049927 :topic/id #uuid "1f1f7b83-a1f4-5b1c-888f-a20ae593bd72" :topic/math-academy-id 1813} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121006 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{x^3-2x}{x^2+4x}\n$$\n\nIf we try to evaluate the limit by directly substituting $x=0$, we get a confusing result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)\\frac{x^{3} - 2x}{x^{2} + 4x} &= ((0)^{3} - 2(0))/((0)^{2} + 4(0)) = \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 0 0\n$$\n\nis called an **indeterminate form** because we are unable to determine the value of the limit from this expression.\n\nHowever, if we simplify the fraction before evaluating the limit, we obtain a more clear result. To simplify the fraction, we can factor the numerator and denominator and then cancel out any common factors.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{x^3-2x}{x^2+4x} & = \\dfrac{x(x^2-2)}{x(x+4)} \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac{\\cancel{x}(x^2-2)}{\\cancel{x}(x+4)} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{x^2-2}{x+4} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the limit of the simplified fraction.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{x^3-2x}{x^2+4x} &= \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{x^2-2}{x+4} \\\\ & = \\dfrac{(0)^2-2}{(0)+4} \\\\ & = \\dfrac{-2}{4} \\\\ & = -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "358e387e-1f79-5799-a6bc-d9a94fd46dd9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 246 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ef3410b5-607f-5268-83e7-58a7aeef5936" :step/math-academy-id 444 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120904}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119937 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119677 :question/id #uuid "7a33db07-63f3-5f8a-b2cd-ca662b91e063" :question/math-academy-id "e-71"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2072b915-15f9-5296-96dd-707e38df3d10" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050323}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522699}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function With One Quadratic Term by Factoring"} :step/id #uuid "213f9703-950a-5e68-9e6e-75e4744f5178" :step/math-academy-id 445 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120911}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119938 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119680 :question/id #uuid "81aac71f-0559-51f5-9567-928d99c08c77" :question/math-academy-id "e-72"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eaa6cbd-46e1-5681-b536-06d16e8d3654" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049153}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519551}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function With Higher Order Terms by Factoring"} :step/id #uuid "2b405cc4-9d67-53b0-94ff-33f5133c2609" :step/math-academy-id 448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120911 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119942 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119681 :question/id #uuid "0c0f5ce6-a84b-51a8-ac60-c557966ec85c" :question/math-academy-id "e-73"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bea72566-55ba-582e-802c-e5ce5ef4a9fc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519532} {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218