-1ae7b5260460" :question/math-academy-id "q-101158" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{t\\to 2}\\frac{{t}^{2}+t-6}{{t}^{2}-9t+14}.$" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{t}^{2}+t-6}{{t}^{2}-9t+14} & =\\frac{{2}^{2}+2-6}{{2}^{2}-9(2)+14}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{t}^{2}+t-6}{{t}^{2}-9t+14} & =\\frac{(t+3)(t-2)}{(t-2)(t-7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{t+3}{t-7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\frac{{t}^{2}+t-6}{{t}^{2}-9t+14} & ={lim}_{t\\to 2}\\frac{t+3}{t-7} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+3}{2-7} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(-5)} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "481c4610-a5dc-53ed-ae6e-370e534c6b45" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518293}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518274 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8a68754-36ce-52d6-9127-21d7b91302be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517899}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517899} :answer-field/id #uuid "ceec04e3-5f86-53fd-a80b-d4bd7eba9ecc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c9bfaabb-1ddb-5591-87c5-e59f3a52dfbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-338200" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{y\\to 4}\\frac{{y}^{2}-16}{{y}^{2}-4y}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to 4}\\frac{{y}^{2}-16}{{y}^{2}-4y} & =\\frac{(4)^{2}-16}{(4)^{2}-4(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{16-16}{16-16} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}-16}{{y}^{2}-4y} & =\\frac{(y-4)(y+4)}{y(y-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{y+4}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to 4}\\frac{{y}^{2}-16}{{y}^{2}-4y} & ={lim}_{y\\to 4}\\frac{y+4}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+4}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "955c3886-484b-5898-8b9e-865782ff28a4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518286}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518286 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79457547-f0fb-58fa-a2c2-317026062441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517716}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517716} :answer-field/id #uuid "288b4964-15f8-5bdf-aea5-937b1035cccb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7eccf962-783f-535d-b46e-1c5922c7413f" :question/math-academy-id "q-338206" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{7{y}^{6}-3{y}^{4}}{5{y}^{5}+10{y}^{4}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{7{y}^{6}-3{y}^{4}}{5{y}^{5}+10{y}^{4}} & =\\frac{7(0)^{6}-3(0)^{4}}{5(0)^{5}+10(0)^{4}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7{y}^{6}-3{y}^{4}}{5{y}^{5}+10{y}^{4}} & =\\frac{{y}^{4}(7{y}^{2}-3)}{{y}^{4}(5y+10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{7{y}^{2}-3}{5y+10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{7{y}^{6}-3{y}^{4}}{5{y}^{5}+10{y}^{4}} & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{7{y}^{2}-3}{5y+10} \\\\  & =\\frac{7(0)-3}{5(0)+10} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b56c33d1-61b7-5263-b02b-f1a42ae44b24"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518293 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518149 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f3291a2-9375-59ea-b11b-d3d51ab51141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517542} {:answer/id #uuid "2974ec32-c354-5379-8e1b-6a9acfaaddc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517562} {:answer/id #uuid "58abb328-ad36-5cec-a8bc-f5da4562671f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517652} {:answer/id #uuid "70d0b790-1084-5fd6-8c21-af30bf9f25cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517705} {:answer/id #uuid "8ae9eecc-f45a-5e19-a6c0-5c2967aa26fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517748}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517562} :answer-field/id #uuid "97ef3454-a8d7-5dce-ad87-6a661e06cf5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "63acba77-7b2e-522a-aba2-29e686142675" :question/math-academy-id "q-140415" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3x+12}{{x}^{3}+64}.$" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3x+12}{{x}^{3}+64} & =\\frac{3(-4)+12}{(-4)^{3}+64}=\\frac{-12+12}{-64+64}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. \n\nNotice that the denominator is a sum of cubes. Therefore, factoring our expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x+12}{{x}^{3}+64} & =\\frac{3(x+4)}{{x}^{3}+{4}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(x+4)}{(x+4)({x}^{2}-4x+{4}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-4x+16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3x+12}{{x}^{3}+64} & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-4x+16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(-4)^{2}-4(-4)+16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16+16+16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c854a8d0-adb7-5877-8a8d-92ee782e3584" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518274}}] :activity/title "Calculating Limits of Rational Functions by Factoring" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517476}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519668} :activity/id #uuid "51893726-ddac-5484-9f3a-d915a01dfdcb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049927 :topic/id #uuid "1f1f7b83-a1f4-5b1c-888f-a20ae593bd72" :topic/math-academy-id 1813} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519641 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c83a02a-d899-55c8-a94f-5215ee121e17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519111} {:answer/id #uuid "81e207e3-fbc2-5800-abe0-1ceefd387562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519195} {:answer/id #uuid "89b85bb3-22c0-5d24-a29b-0c275ecfe2a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519215} {:answer/id #uuid "d56cb5bc-4d9d-5419-a948-dca0397cf746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519344} {:answer/id #uuid "e10ffd2c-df31-5361-8cae-6b39de43c35c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519365}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519215} :answer-field/id #uuid "be118eae-f956-5136-8758-541234de46d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2778d4f8-72c7-5929-98a7-5df304873786" :question/math-academy-id "q-101160" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{y\\to 3}\\frac{{y}^{2}+3y-18}{{y}^{2}-3y}.$" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to 3}\\frac{{y}^{2}+3y-18}{{y}^{2}-3y} & =\\frac{{3}^{2}+3(3)-18}{{3}^{2}-3(3)}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}+3y-18}{{y}^{2}-3y} & =\\frac{(y+6)(y-3)}{y(y-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{y+6}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to 3}\\frac{{y}^{2}+3y-18}{{y}^{2}-3y} & ={lim}_{y\\to 3}\\frac{y+6}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+6}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{3} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6d3de455-eb8b-5f64-94cd-7656cf858b93" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519662}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519662 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519542 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b385cd1-b9ce-5705-9e8c-ff293413d49c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519185}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519185} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd8d554e-6195-547e-bcb1-ff6fe5d58358" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5986bf81-4caa-5e55-8c99-36ab322d7c9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-313149" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -2}\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-14}{x+2}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-14}{x+2} & =\\frac{(-2)^{2}-5(-2)-14}{-2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+10-14}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-14}{x+2} & =\\frac{(x-7)(x+2)}{x+2} \\\\  & =x-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}(x-7) & =(-2)-7 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c8ebbc60-a13e-5fb9-bd31-db31053b64b4"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519668 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e1be6ba-0318-5199-9cf7-6cfc2c56fa02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519156} {:answer/id #uuid "892f567d-b4cc-5f65-95a7-7373905d09c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519211} {:answer/id #uuid "8ce5d32b-9d7b-5303-97bb-68e862c5ae64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519222} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c6b966-6937-5cda-a2a8-2103c125ed81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519266} {:answer/id #uuid "edf46e72-237e-5ade-b7c8-d89a1c8c5d81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519382}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519266} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b3845e7-e81e-5c5b-85b8-7d7f11e07468" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "72828711-fc6d-5cc1-8d14-0b819a351e61" :question/math-academy-id "q-3749" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{2}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{-2(1)^{4}+6(1)^{2}-4}{(1)^{2}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-2{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{-2({x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+2)}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2({x}^{2}-2)({x}^{2}-1)}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =-2({x}^{2}-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{2}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\,\\,-2({x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =-2(1-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ec795ac2-9c40-5579-8d71-0f6e0fe67464" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519641}}] :activity/title "Calculating Limits of Rational Functions by Factoring" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518978}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125668} :activity/id #uuid "55d83791-4999-5136-909d-35e46a8ed919" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050004 :topic/id #uuid "25876b80-efb1-53ed-9327-08d34c32cc9a" :topic/math-academy-id 1903} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125668 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125743 :tutorial/content "To calculate the limit at infinity of a rational function, such as\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{x^4}{x^5-x^2}\\right),\n$$\n\n we apply the following procedure:\n\n**Step 1:** Divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the *denominator*.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}-{x}^{2}}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{4}÷{x}^{5}}{({x}^{5}-{x}^{2})÷{x}^{5}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}}}{\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\frac{1}{x}}{1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Apply the rule that if $n$ is any positive integer then $\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^n}\\right)=0.$ Evaluate the remaining expression to obtain the answer.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}-{x}^{2}}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\frac{1}{x}}{1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ed558480-81a6-5baf-ab3a-f19468f77d72" :tutorial/math-academy-id 258 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6cb8d91a-6de6-5bd6-878e-78ec12b7fedf" :step/math-academy-id 471 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125669}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125669 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124661 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124392 :question/id #uuid "0fc616fa-c901-53ff-acab-cdada6bb5ce2" :question/math-academy-id "e-80"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32cbf366-20bd-5a52-a14f-83add698bf07" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048515}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486408}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Rational Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "eb8b99ee-a21a-5d00-8571-260574714375" :step/math-academy-id 472 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125670}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125670 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124662 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124394 :question/id #uuid "b648ed80-e2e1-57c4-bd37-147d92315e90" :question/math-academy-id "e-81"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "020c3de3-bcaf-5ade-ba50-2f15e344157d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049637}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522734}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Infinite Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "98d0d8f6-bd8d-5c4f-811f-469eaac31888" :step/math-academy-id 475 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125671}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125671 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125748 :tutorial/content "The horizontal asymptotes of a rational function $y=f(x)$ coincide with the infinite limits of $f(x).$\n\nFor example, we can use the same procedure as before to find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x) = \\dfrac{x+1}{x^2+2}.$ As a result, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(1+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+0}{1+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the horizontal asymptote of the graph $y=f(x)$ is $y=0$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/27/27c77f2d6a06bad338982d000f916811201c45e9b16713caf40cc4d5bcf911bd.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "298d636c-df6c-580c-adce-c09a77dec64a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 261 :tutorial/title "Horizontal Asymptotes of Rational Functions"} :step/id #uuid "22bf77c7-a859-5972-9d3a-30c0590708ed" :step/math-academy-id 477 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125672}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125672 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124663 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124395 :question/id #uuid "c3798fe4-058f-5970-a903-65c654abb788" :question/math-academy-id "e-82"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee15db10-b92c-5f48-8983-e602068c7e08" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049037}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323682} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503740}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Asymptote of a Rational Function"} :step/id #uuid "f59b9e85-79cc-5fcd-b7a2-da25e2aeac81" :step/math-academy-id 478 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125673}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125673 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124664 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124408 :question/id #uuid "32a8e4bb-4191-55a8-883b-baa36f67b061" :question/math-academy-id "e-83"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bde258b7-1654-5e73-8703-0e42371db8f4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486415}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Rational Function has No Horizontal Asymptote"} :step/id #uuid "f3b88a81-a304-57e3-a798-5432c418d285" :step/math-academy-id 480}] :activity/title "Limits at Infinity and Horizontal Asymptotes of Rational Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124703}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418090} :activity/id #uuid "b4f97133-82cd-5750-be9b-b95616155e6b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050004 :topic/id #uuid "25876b80-efb1-53ed-9327-08d34c32cc9a" :topic/math-academy-id 1903} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413323 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0051830d-55e5-5121-b11a-0d87da7f40fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413301} {:answer/id #uuid "0e733fa1-bbd0-5f83-a1a9-64ce04aed72b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413302} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b385fb-ca34-55cc-a656-aaa065330917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413311} {:answer/id #uuid "cad17541-13af-57a0-9141-29e20640a0ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413313} {:answer/id #uuid "db92d19b-e030-57a9-b3fd-2d1169f5e7a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413315}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413313} :answer-field/id #uuid "36e0c9a9-15c6-51ff-ac4a-304c0aa988be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e2d47372-f9cb-5c02-86fd-148b7f1d18b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-101320" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}+1}{{x}^{3}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{4}+1}{{x}^{3}-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x+0)}{(1-0)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."} :step/id #uuid "37331294-019e-50e4-a478-f8952797d0e6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418088}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418088 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413321 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20243682-29c5-5259-864f-3c811fbe7f7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413304} {:answer/id #uuid "4f14c146-ddb9-57bd-ad6f-3930420ac403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413306} {:answer/id #uuid "95bed720-0647-52ed-9834-06d42bf8de74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413310} {:answer/id #uuid "b4ee551d-2ce2-5847-a076-aa2475baa979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413312} {:answer/id #uuid "f405f918-ce19-52f9-9c3d-f68c97ea5cd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413316}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413312} :answer-field/id #uuid "e22bffa8-51cb-5c7c-a8dc-24d845160961" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "88ec9ee8-0b06-53f7-bc3c-92317b5059e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-101322" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{9{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-x}{5{x}^{2}+3}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-x}{5{x}^{2}+3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{9{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(9x+1-\\frac{1}{x})}{(5+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(9x+1-0)}{(5+0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(9x+1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."} :step/id #uuid "401b3af7-fa57-5cc4-9500-1387b9727f71" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418089}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418089 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413322 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "955a14ee-2d66-50bc-8de3-377077da9e48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413309}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413309} :answer-field/id #uuid "e59f45ed-d037-5bd6-940e-13f41f6c836c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d8cdb7dd-976e-5b44-901a-f1aa1b8415b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-339615" :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{7}+3}{{x}^{4}+6x+2}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{7}+3}{{x}^{4}+6x+2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{-{x}^{7}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{6x}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{3}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})}{(1+\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\text{ }\\frac{-{x}^{3}+0}{1+0+0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,-{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "75cf104c-743b-5715-8fba-baa298a50d9a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418090 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124919 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f73f57-316e-5fb3-bc4a-e767aaf24db8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413303} {:answer/id #uuid "30464325-d158-5e08-93a8-e607ad63b4a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413305} {:answer/id #uuid "521202c4-e823-5b6e-92ea-799e96ebcde5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413307} {:answer/id #uuid "9123a10f-7315-5079-b272-8ae329eb3b75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413308} {:answer/id #uuid "cf5c50b9-a9ea-50a2-a08b-b1461f5b7bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413314}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413307} :answer-field/id #uuid "381810c0-51a5-5b82-b7b6-6ce8ed23d924" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a9648fae-8c3b-5e03-83c5-38b5774f94d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-35828" :question/problem "What is the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ with $f(x)=\\frac{3x+4}{6-15x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3x+4}{6-15x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{4}{x})}{(\\frac{6}{x}-\\frac{15x}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(3+\\frac{4}{x})}{(\\frac{6}{x}-15)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+0}{0-15} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-\\frac{1}{5}.$"} :step/id #uuid "812c3262-08eb-552d-a220-5cf16c367fb2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418091}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418091 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335944 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0948bf66-b9eb-50e0-aa36-a645d1dd4d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333074} {:answer/id #uuid "7f2618e2-1548-5494-9496-e1c7e62150fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334121} {:answer/id #uuid "89c810fb-1fda-543b-a4ab-03e4899ffff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334214} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d2b174-a819-5c3c-8807-a772dccc544a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Do